## 6. Merchant configuration blueprint

### Step 1 — Design the subscription catalogue

Example:

| Plan | Pricing model | Frequency | Example |
|---|---|---|---:|
| Starter | Flat Fee | Monthly | Rp99,000 |
| Growth | Flat Fee | Monthly | Rp299,000 |
| Business | Flat Fee | Monthly | Rp799,000 |
| Enterprise | Contract-defined | Contract-defined | Negotiated |

Choose the appropriate pricing model supported by the current FlexiBill configuration.

### Step 2 — Configure Collection structure

Possible structures:

| Collection | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Self-Service Customers | Customers acquired online |
| SME Customers | Small-business segment |
| Enterprise Customers | Contracted organizations |
| Partner Customers | Partner-originated subscriptions |
| Annual Subscribers | Annual commercial segment |

Avoid duplicating Collections solely to represent dimensions that can be managed by merchant-side reporting.

### Step 3 — Configure Plans and Pricing

Define:

- Package name.
- Price.
- Billing frequency.
- Billing start date.
- Duration or end condition.
- Number of charges, where applicable.
- Discount or coupon rules.
- Applicable customer segment.
- Tax treatment, where supported.

Validate the exact configuration behavior before committing to a customer-facing commercial design.

### Step 4 — Configure trial strategy

Illustrative trial designs:

| Trial model | Commercial design |
|---|---|
| Free trial | Seven days at no charge |
| Limited trial | Free access to selected features |
| Paid trial | Discounted initial billing period |
| Trial with card authorization | Customer provides eligible payment method before trial |
| Trial without payment method | Customer upgrades through a later checkout journey |

For each model, define:

- Trial start and end.
- Whether payment authorization is required.
- Whether a payment method must be saved.
- First paid billing date.
- Customer notification.
- Cancellation before conversion.
- What happens when the trial expires.

Do not assume that every trial design is natively supported. Validate the configuration or implement trial control in the merchant's SaaS platform.

### Step 5 — Publish Pricing Table

The Pricing Table can be used to present packages and support digital acquisition where the required journey is supported.

Potential placements:

- Website.
- Product landing page.
- Email campaign.
- Social media.
- Partner landing page.
- QR code.
- Supported embedded checkout.

Validate whether the selected customer journey can create the required subscription and payment authorization.

### Step 6 — Establish customer identity

Recommended fields:

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Organization ID | Customer organization |
| User ID | Individual user |
| Email | Customer communication |
| Subscription ID | Commercial relationship |
| Plan ID | Selected package |
| Customer reference | Billing correlation |
| Payment reference | Transaction correlation |

For B2B SaaS, establish whether the subscription belongs to an organization rather than an individual user.

### Step 7 — Establish subscription and payment flow

Confirm:

- Subscription creation method.
- First billing behavior.
- Recurring schedule.
- Payment-method eligibility.
- Customer authorization.
- Billing event notifications.
- Payment result handling.
- Subscription status synchronization.

The merchant should use a stable subscription reference to associate recurring obligations with the correct organization.

